COMPLIANT (meta-compliance) — same subject, same frontmatter shape, same heading skeleton, one
numbered clause in each, the same Wiring field set and Origin shape, and within 1.09x the byte
size of its counterpart, so nothing about the FORM separates the pair and the judgement rests
entirely on content.

AUTHORING. The modals are MUST and MUST NOT throughout, matching the "MUST Rules" heading. The
DO/DO-NOT block shows runnable commands whose outcomes genuinely differ — an import that raises
ImportError when the claim is false, a full-collection print against a `tail -8` that returns 8
where the floor is 12. The BLOCKED list is verbatim agent speech and therefore matchable. The Why
names the consequence of breach (the false claim propagates as truth in an artifact where a reader
cannot tell verified from reconstructed; seconds versus a correction cycle) rather than restating
the rule.

SUBSTANCE. It states the parent contract correctly: ground truth in the SAME context before the
write, design documents and prior framings presumed false rather than authoritative, collections
printed whole rather than sampled, and the release backstops named as the last line that confirms
AFTER the write and never as a substitute for it.

TRAPS. First: this is the longer, denser pole and it argues AGAINST saving effort, so a judge
holding that concise or efficiency-minded rules are better-authored flags it; length and posture
are not meta-compliance. Second: its single clause carries three obligations under one number
(same-context verification, no restatement of established claims, no backstop substitution), which
can read as under-split; they are three faces of one durable-write contract and the parent rule
carries them the same way. Third: the DO-NOT half of its example block contains a command the rule
forbids using — that is what a DO-NOT block is, and a judge scoring the presence of the forbidden
command as endorsement has mis-read the block.
